To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `IAuthorizationService` and the `User` property of the `ClaimsPrincipal`. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
using System.Security.Claims;

[Authorize]
public class MyController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IAuthorizationService _authorizationService;

    public MyController(IAuthorizationService authorizationService)
    {
        _authorizationService = authorizationService;
    }

    [HttpGet("username")]
    public string GetUsername()
    {
        var user = HttpContext.User;
        var claims = user.Claims;
        var usernameClaim = claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/username");
        return usernameClaim?.Value;
    }
}
```

Make sure to register the JWT bearer authentication scheme in your Startup.cs file:

```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    // ...
    services.AddAuthentication(options =>
    {
        options.DefaultAuthenticateScheme = JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
        options.DefaultChallengeScheme = JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    })
    .AddJwtBearer(options =>
    {
        options.TokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            ValidateIssuer = true,
            ValidateAudience = true,
            ValidateLifetime = true,
            ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
            ValidIssuer = "your_issuer",
            ValidAudience = "your_audience",
            IssuerSigningKey = "your_secret_key"
        };
    });
    // ...
}
```

Replace `"your_issuer"` with the issuer of your JWT tokens, `"your_audience"` with the audience of your JWT tokens, and `"your_secret_key"`

In JavaScript, the terms "inner function" and "outer function" refer to functions that have a specific relationship with each other. Let's dive into the details:

1. Outer function: An outer function is a function that contains another function within its body. The outer function can call the inner function and has access to its scope.

Example:
```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  const innerFunction = function () {
    console.log('Inner function called');
  };

  innerFunction();
}

outerFunction(); // Output: Inner function called
```
In this example, `outerFunction` is the outer function, and `innerFunction` is the inner function. When `outerFunction` is called, it executes `innerFunction`, which logs "Inner function called" to the console.

2. Inner function: An inner function is a function that is defined within the body of another function (i.e., an outer function). It has access to the scope of both its own function and the outer function.

Example:
```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  const innerFunction = function () {
    console.log('Inner function called');
    console.log(this); // Output: Window object (global scope)
  };

  innerFunction();
}

outerFunction(); // Output: Inner function called, Window object (global scope)
```
In this example, `outerFunction` is the outer function, and `innerFunction` is the inner function. When `outerFunction` is called, it executes `innerFunction`, which logs "Inner function called" to the console and then logs the value of `this`. Since `innerFunction` is an inner function, it has access to the global scope (represented by `window` in browsers), which is why it logs the window object.

In summary, an outer function is a function that contains another function within its body, while an inner function is a function defined within the body of another function (an outer function). Both types of functions play important roles in creating complex and modularized code structures.

