Here's a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and returns `false` if it's not set to `true`. If `NODE_ENV` is set to `true`, it calls the next middleware function.

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvMiddleware implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv !== 'true') {
      return false;
    }

    return true;
  }
}
```

You can use this decorator in your Nest.js application by adding it to the `middleware` array of your route or controller. For example:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseInterceptors } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvMiddleware } from './env.middleware';

@Controller('api')
export class ApiController {
  @Get()
  @UseInterceptors(new EnvMiddleware())
  getHello(): string {
    return 'Hello World!';
  }
}
```

This will ensure that the `getHello` method is only called if the `NODE_ENV` variable is set to `true`. Otherwise, it will return `false`.

